# SABOTAGE: A4 (ADR-047 §5.3 / RP-AG-5; ADR-043 §6.2) gate sabotage — THE crown-jewel
# defeat: an `effect: :write` agent tool EXECUTES INLINE in the turn, with the agent's own
# run authority, instead of opening an E3 approval and parking. No approve event, no
# distinct human, no approver actor — exactly the "AI writes" ADR-043 §6.2 rules do not
# exist. The write branch of tool_turn/10 is deleted so a write falls through to the READ
# path and fires `entry.module.run/2` as the run's OWNER scope. The named RP-AG-5 red (the
# record is UNCHANGED, the run PARKS, only an approval exists) must flip; the
# approve-then-execute positive control flips with it (there is no approval left to
# approve), proving the mechanism is gone rather than merely mis-ordered.
# APP: samen_core
# TEST_FILES: test/ai/agent_write_test.exs
# MUST_FAIL: a WRITE tool call does NOT execute
diff --git a/samen_core/lib/samen/ai/agent.ex b/samen_core/lib/samen/ai/agent.ex
--- a/samen_core/lib/samen/ai/agent.ex
+++ b/samen_core/lib/samen/ai/agent.ex
@@ -887,27 +887,10 @@
          replayed?
        ) do
     case authorize_tool(tools, kind, args) do
-      # A4 (ADR-047 §5.3; ADR-043 §6.2 unamended): a MUTATING tool never executes here.
-      # It passed the same four-way intersection a read tool passes — and that admission
-      # buys it a PROPOSAL, not an execution. The decision stamp lands first (so the
-      # args digest that BINDS the proposal is committed before anything else), then the
-      # E3 approval opens and the run parks. `entry.module.run/2` is not called on this
-      # path at all; the ONLY caller of it for a write is `execute_approved/3`, after a
-      # distinct human approved, with the APPROVER's actor.
-      {:ok, %{effect: :write} = entry, normalized} ->
-        turn_row = decide_tool!(turn_row, entry.kind, normalized)
-
-        propose_turn(
